ssed pawn to a3."],"better":"15.a3 pushes the passed pawn to a3 and takes b4 away from the knight on c6 and the rook on b8. Engine line: 15…Nd4 16.Ne2 Nb3 17.Rb1 Nxc1 18.Nxc1 c4 (−1.02)."},{"ply":32,"name":"16…Rxf3","cls":"brilliant","color":"b","headline":"16…Rxf3!! A real sacrifice: Black gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the rook on f3 where it can be taken.","White can take, but after 17.gxf3 Nxe5 18.Qe2 Nf4 the engine rates it −3.73.","Takes the knight on f3.","16…Rxf3 takes the knight on f3.","White answers 17.gxf3, winning a rook for a piece and a pawn.","Engine line: 17.gxf3 Nxe5 18.Qe2 Nf4 19.Qa6 Nxf3+ 20.Kf1 (−3.73)."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"16…Rxf3!! offers the rook on f3. Taking it gives Black the material back and more."},{"title":"What Black gives up","text":"16…Rxf3 puts the rook on f3 where it can be taken by the g2 pawn. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It takes the knight on f3."},{"title":"If White takes the rook","text":"17.gxf3 is met by 17…Nxe5, taking the e5 pawn and threatening …Nxf3+, winning the queen for a knight. Then 18…Nxf3 takes the f3 pawn, and 19…exd5 takes the knight on d5. Material is level and Black is clearly better (−2.73). This is White's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 16…Nd4, is clearly weaker (−3.13 against −3.73). White answers 17.a3, attacking the rook on b4. Black would still be winning, but far less than after 16…Rxf3."}]},{"ply":38,"name":"19…Nf4","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"19…Nf4! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The next best move, 19…Qb8, would drop Black's winning chances from 80% to 66% (−1.82).","19…Nf4 threatens …Nxe2+, winning the queen, and stops Nb5.","It threatens …Nxe2+, winning the queen, so White has to spend the next move on it.","White was threatening Nb5, winning a knight for a pawn, picking it up with 21.Qxf3. Nf4 takes care of it.","The engine's plan continues with …Qb8 and …Nxf3: this move is the first step.","White answers 20.Qa6."]},{"ply":46,"name":"23…Qb7","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"23…Qb7! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Attacks the knight on d5, which is undefended.","Moves the attacked queen to safety.","The next best move, 23…Nxe2, would drop Black's winning chances from 94% to 82% (−4.21).","23…Qb7 threatens …Qxd5, winning the queen, and gets the queen out of the way of Nxc7.","It threatens …Qxd5, winning the queen, so White has to spend the next move on it.","White was threatening Nxc7, winning a knight. The queen steps away."]}],"opening":{"name":"Blumenfeld Countergambit","eco":"E10","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 8.Nc3: 15 moves in all, 8 by White and 7 by Black.","Theory runs out after 8.Nc3: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}